      "output": "# GYROID ring: notes\n\n**Delivered:** `ring.glb` is published to *model* and `ring.jpg` to *render*. Both were built and checked in the live Blender 5.2.3 session. All figures below were read back from the files and the scene.\n\n## Lattice\n```\nawwo_param.minimal.sheet('gyroid', size=(0.0225, 0.0225, 0.0075), cells=(18, 2, 3),\n    thickness=0.00045, resolution=160, shape='ring', inner=0.0173, wrap=True)\n```\n- **Settings:** this is the house's size 7 example unchanged: 18 cells round, 2 across the band, 3 up it, 0.45 mm walls, resolution 160, `min_part` 0.02 and `smooth` 12 left at their defaults.\n- **Blender object:** made with `awwo_param.blender.mesh_object(mesh, 'Ring_Gyroid', material=Gold18k, smooth=True)`. It was not thickened, remeshed or subdivided.\n- **Wall test:** I tried 0.40 mm walls once. The lattice looked lacier but no better, and that is right at the casting minimum, so I went back to 0.45 mm.\n- **Measured size:** 17.34 mm inside diameter, 22.46 mm outside, 7.41 mm wide. The band is about 2.56 mm thick. The width comes out 0.09 mm under the nominal 7.5 mm, probably because the generator rounds the edges.\n- **Render mesh:** 1,498,956 triangles and 747,386 vertices.\n\n## Render\n- **Engine:** Cycles on the GPU at 192 samples with denoising, 1600×1600, JPEG at quality 90, AgX with Medium High Contrast, exposure 0. The final render took 23 s.\n- **Gold:** Principled BSDF with metallic 1, roughness 0.15, base colour (1.0, 0.75, 0.36) in linear and a warm specular tint.\n- **Set:** a near-black charcoal velvet sweep (rough, with a little sheen and a fine noise bump).\n- **Lights:**\n  - Key: a 0.25 m disc area light (1.6 W, spread 50°), up and to the left.\n  - Rim: a 12 mm × 200 mm strip behind and to the right (2 W).\n  - Fill: a warm 0.2 m disc (0.06 W).\n- **Camera:** 100 mm lens, 0.115 m away, 14° above. The ring stands upright, turned 38° for the three-quarter view. Depth of field is off, so the whole band is sharp.\n- **File:** `ring.jpg` is 1600×1600 and 290,686 bytes (284 KiB).\n\n## What each look at a render changed\n1. **test1:** the floor and wall were far too bright and grey, and the ring was framed too small. I moved the back wall from −0.08 m to −0.22 m and darkened the velvet. I moved the camera in from 0.135 m to 0.115 m and warmed the gold slightly.\n2. **diag** (sweep hidden): this showed that the ring itself was framed and lit correctly, so the grey came from the sweep.\n3. **test2:** the backdrop was still mid-grey. I cut the velvet sheen from 1 to 0.35, darkened it again, narrowed the key's spread, and tried 0.40 mm walls.\n4. **test3:** the backdrop was still light, so the lights were too strong overall. I lowered the key from 6 to 1.6 W, the rim from 3 to 2 W and the fill from 0.25 to 0.06 W, and returned to 0.45 mm walls.\n5. **test4:** the gold read well, but the velvet was still dove-grey. I lowered the sheen to 0.12.\n6. **test5:** the velvet was still too light. Its specular was the cause, so I lowered its specular level to 0.08 and the glossy bounces from 8 to 5.\n7. **test6:** this gave the right deep charcoal sweep and a bright rim on the edges. The gold leaned a little pale, so I made it slightly more yellow (0.75, 0.36).\n8. **Final, ring.jpg (192 samples):** the ring sits whole in the frame and is sharp. Highlights are bright but not blown and the gold is warm yellow. I made no further change.\n\n## GLB\n- **Mesh:** a copy of the lattice (`Ring_Gyroid_web`) reduced with a Decimate modifier (collapse, ratio 0.045). The render mesh was not touched.\n- **Export:** applied on export, with no Draco or meshopt compression. The file has no `extensionsUsed`, only POSITION and NORMAL attributes, and one node.\n- **Material:** PBR metallic-roughness, base colour (1, 0.75, 0.36), metallic 1 (the default), roughness 0.15.\n- **Triangles:** 67,452.\n- **Scale:** metres. The bounds read back from the GLB accessors are ±0.01120 × ±0.00372 × ±0.01120 m, so the band axis is the glTF Y axis, upright in a Y-up viewer.\n- **File size:** 1,165,024 bytes (1.11 MiB), under the 1.5 MiB limit.\n\n## Checks run\n- Mesh bounds and inside/outside diameters measured from the vertices.\n- World bounding box checked to confirm the ring rests on the floor.\n- The GLB header and JSON parsed for triangle count, bounds, extensions and material; file sizes read back.\n- Every render viewed with workspace_view.\n\n## Not verified\n- I did not run a separate watertight or manifold check; that relies on the generator's documentation.\n- I did not load the GLB in the site's viewer.\n- `scene.py` rebuilds the scene and render; the decimated web copy and the GLB export were run directly in the session and are not in it yet.",
