      "output": "# GYROID: pendant and earrings\n\nBoth pieces were built in the live Blender 5.2.3 from `scene.py`, which is saved in the workspace and rebuilds each scene. I read `help(awwo_param.minimal.sheet)` before building anything. Each lattice is one `awwo_param.blender.mesh_object(mesh, material=gold, smooth=True)`. Neither was thickened, remeshed or subdivided. All four files are published, and they total about 3.3 MiB.\n\n## The two sheet() calls\n- **Pendant:** `sheet('schwarz_d', size=(0.020, 0.020, 0.020), cells=(6, 6, 6), thickness=0.0005, resolution=110, shape='sphere')`. This is the brief exactly. By eye it reads as a fine, even lattice, so I changed nothing.\n- **Earrings:** `sheet('gyroid', size=(0.016, 0.016, 0.003), cells=(4, 4, 0.75), thickness=0.00045, resolution=160, shape='cylinder')`. The 0.75 cell through the 3 mm thickness was my choice; the brief gave no depth count. By eye the disc reads as an open woven lattice, neither dense nor sparse, so I changed nothing. Both earrings share this one lattice mesh. All walls are 0.45 mm or more.\n\n## Measured sizes (bounding boxes in Blender)\n- **Pendant:** lattice 19.8 × 19.8 × 19.8 mm (about 20 mm across).\n  - Bail: round wire 1.2 mm thick, 5.0 mm across, its centre set 0.8 mm into the top of the sphere so it is fused in.\n  - Cable chain: 24 links of 0.34 mm wire (2.4 × 1.75 mm links, each turned 90° to the last), rising to 61.7 mm above the centre.\n  - Whole piece: 19.8 × 19.8 × 71.6 mm.\n- **Earrings:** lattice 16.0 mm across, 3.0 mm thick.\n  - Bezel: an oval-section rim, 0.9 mm wide and 3.4 mm deep, over the cut edge (the disc with its rim is 17.2 mm across).\n  - A small jump ring, 0.8 mm wire, joins the bezel to the hook.\n  - French hook: 0.8 mm wire, about 25 mm tall.\n  - Second earring: a mirror copy (scale −1 in X), turned 24° about the vertical against −16° for the first, and tilted slightly differently.\n  - Pair as shot: 39.8 × 14.8 × 34.5 mm.\n\n## Triangle counts\n| | Render mesh | GLB |\n|---|---|---|\n| Pendant lattice | 2,269,056 | 45,380 (Decimate modifier, collapse, ratio 0.02, on a copy) |\n| Pendant bail | 4,608 | 4,608 |\n| Pendant chain | 23,040 | 11,520 (collapse 0.5) |\n| **Pendant GLB total** | | **61,508** |\n| Earring lattice, each | 861,796 | 25,852 (collapse 0.03, on a copy) |\n| Bezel / ring / hook, each earring | 10,240 / 2,048 / 2,416 | the same |\n| **Earrings GLB total (both)** | | **81,112** |\n\nThe render meshes were never decimated.\n\n## GLB files\nBoth were checked by parsing the GLB header and JSON with `check_glb.py`.\n- **pendant.glb:** 1,096,948 bytes (1.05 MiB). Positions run about ±0.0099 m and up to 0.0617 m, so the scale is real metres.\n- **earrings.glb:** 1,486,280 bytes (1.42 MiB), both earrings in one file. Positions are in metres (the pair spans 0.039 m).\n- Neither file uses any extension (no Draco, no meshopt).\n- One PBR material, `18k_Yellow_Gold`: base colour (1.0, 0.74, 0.30), metallic 1, roughness 0.15.\n\n## Renders\n- Cycles on the GPU, 160 samples with adaptive sampling, OptiX denoising.\n- 1600×1600 JPEG at quality 90, AgX Medium High Contrast, exposure −0.8. Glossy bounces were 8, then 4.\n- Gold: Principled BSDF, metallic 1, roughness 0.15, base colour (1.0, 0.74, 0.30).\n- Set: a charcoal velvet sweep (sheen, fine noise bump), a 16 cm square soft key, two narrow strip rim lights, a faint warm fill (colour 1.0/0.80/0.58) and a soft top card. The lights are hidden from the camera.\n- Camera: no depth of field, so every part is sharp. Pendant lens 180 mm, earrings 118 mm.\n- pendant.jpg is 415,085 bytes and earrings.jpg 301,940 bytes; both were read back as 1600×1600 JPEGs.\n\n## What each look at a render changed\n1. **Pendant preview 1:** the background was too light and grey, and the gold too pale and brassy. I darkened the velvet, made the gold warmer, set exposure to −0.8 and widened the lens framing to 180 mm.\n2. **Pendant preview 2:** the edge of the sweep showed in the frame. I made the sweep wider and taller with a larger curve.\n3. **Pendant at full size, plus a close crop:** the highlights were a little hot and the gold slightly lemon. I lowered the key light by 15% and moved the gold toward a warmer, deeper yellow (1.0, 0.74, 0.30). I cut the velvet sheen.\n4. **Pendant again:** the top highlight was still bright. I lowered the top card from 2.0 to 1.3 and the sheen to 0.3.\n5. **Final pendant check:** the whole piece and the chain rising out of frame are sharp, the lattice is fine and even, and I published it.\n6. **Earrings preview:** framing, the pair and the hooks were all fine, but the bezel looked heavy. I thinned its wire from 1.2 to 0.9 mm wide and moved the jump ring down to match.\n7. **Earrings at full size, plus a crop of the hook and ring:** both pieces are whole and sharp, the hooks thread the rings, the two earrings visibly differ, and I published it.\n\n## Not verified\n- I did not open either GLB in a web viewer (no viewer test was run here).\n- I did not run a separate test that the lattices are watertight and manifold; that rests on `sheet()`'s own documentation.\n- The bail, ring and hook touch or overlap the lattice and bezel but are separate meshes, not joined with booleans. A caster would need to join them before casting.",
