      "output": "# GYROID campaign: photographer's notes\n\nAll three photographs are rendered and published: **hero.jpg** at 1920×1080 (228,443 bytes), **detail.jpg** at 1600×1600 (346,116 bytes) and **pair.jpg** at 1600×1600 (222,952 bytes). I looked at every render with workspace_view before changing anything, and I made a Pillow crop of the macro to check it closer.\n\nTwo things fall short of the brief:\n- **Detail:** the ring's outer face reads clearly, but the lattice holes have a rough, ragged rim. That roughness is in the full-resolution mesh itself, and it shows at this magnification.\n- **Pair:** the stand's round feet still pick up a pale grey highlight on the right foot, against the brief's \"nothing pale in the frame\".\n\nDetails follow under each shot.\n\n## Studio (one Blender call, reused for every shot)\n- **Pieces:** I imported the three GLBs with `bpy.ops.import_scene.gltf` inside a window override. Each is shaded smooth and none of the geometry was changed. For the hero only, the pendant's chain is bent along a curve with a Curve modifier, which changes its shape in that render but not its mesh.\n- **Gold:** one 18k-gold material for every piece, `Gold18k`. It is a Principled BSDF with metallic 1, roughness 0.15 and base colour (1.0, 0.77, 0.33) in linear.\n- **Sweep:** charcoal velvet (`CharcoalVelvet`) with roughness 1, sheen 0.03, specular 0 and a fine noise bump.\n- **Lights:**\n  - Key: a 0.25 m disc area light, spread 28°.\n  - Rim: a narrow 12 × 200 mm strip, 2 W.\n  - Fill: a warm disc, 0.03–0.06 W.\n- **Camera:** one, named `Camera`.\n- **Render settings:** Cycles on the GPU (OptiX, Tesla T4) with denoising, AgX view with Medium High Contrast, JPEG at quality 90.\n  - Tests were half size at 32–64 samples.\n  - Corrected renders used 96 samples.\n  - Finals used 192 samples.\n- **Scripts:** `scene.py`, `hero.py`, `detail.py` and `pair.py` rebuild every shot.\n\n## HERO (1920×1080)\n- **Setup:**\n  - **Camera:** 85 mm lens, 0.27 m from the group, 16° above it and 14° to the left, with a horizontal lens shift of −0.17 so the pieces sit in the right two-thirds.\n  - **Focus:** depth of field off, so every piece is sharp.\n  - **Lights:** key 0.9 W, rim 2 W behind and to the right, fill 0.05 W.\n  - **Arrangement:** the ring stands upright, turned 30°. The pendant lies flat with its chain curling open. The right earring is tilted 16° and rests across the left one.\n- **What each look changed:**\n  1. **test1:** the group was off to the side and the sweep looked grey. I placed the camera by angle and distance, added the lens shift, and darkened the velvet's colour.\n  2. **test1b:** the velvet was still too light. I cut its sheen and specular and narrowed the key's spread.\n  3. **test1c:** the chain ran out of the frame. I turned the pendant, moved the camera in to 0.27 m and moved the key.\n  4. **test1d:** the chain still trailed badly. I replaced its path with an opening spiral so it lies whole on the velvet and never on itself.\n  5. **test1e:** the composition worked. I made the gold slightly more yellow (0.77, 0.33) and narrowed the key to 28°.\n  6. **test2 (full size):** the earrings sat too far right and the front was underlit. I moved the earrings left and brought the key forward.\n  7. **Final:** all three pieces are whole and sharp, and the left third is free for the headline.\n\n## DETAIL (1600×1600)\n- **Ring:** I rebuilt it with the ring maker's exact call: `awwo_param.minimal.sheet('gyroid', size=(0.0225, 0.0225, 0.0075), cells=(18, 2, 3), thickness=0.00045, resolution=160, shape='ring', inner=0.0173, wrap=True)`. That gives 1,498,956 triangles. It has the same gold and the same transform as the imported ring, which is hidden.\n- **Setup:**\n  - **Camera:** 100 mm lens, 44 mm from the band, aimed at the side that faces away from the key, with a horizontal shift of −0.10.\n  - **Focus:** depth of field off.\n  - **Lights and exposure:** key 0.6 W, rim 1.6 W, exposure −0.8.\n  - **Other pieces:** hidden in this render.\n- **What each look changed:**\n  1. **test1:** with f/22 depth of field the far walls were soft; I saw it in the Pillow crop. I switched depth of field off. The highlights were also too hot, so I lowered exposure to −1.3 and turned down the key and rim.\n  2. **test2:** the view was better, but the other pieces showed and the image was too dark. I hid them and raised exposure to −0.5.\n  3. **test3:** the lattice was still too small in the frame. I moved in from 58 to 44 mm, added the shift and set exposure to −0.8.\n  4. **test4:** right, so I rendered the final.\n- **Final:** the walls and rounded edges of the outer face read clearly, but the rims of the holes are rough. That comes from the full-resolution mesh itself, not from decimation or render noise, and is worth showing the ring maker.\n\n## PAIR (1600×1600)\n- **Setup:**\n  - **Stand:** a slim matte-black stand: a 0.7 mm-radius bar 52 mm high on two posts with round feet. The earrings hang 26 mm apart, each hook resting on the bar.\n  - **Camera:** 100 mm lens, 0.215 m away, 8° above, straight on, aimed at 28 mm height.\n  - **Focus:** depth of field off.\n  - **Lights and exposure:** key 0.7 W, rim 2 W, fill 0.03 W, exposure −0.5.\n- **What each look changed:**\n  1. **test1:** the earrings were small, and the stand's feet and posts read pale grey. I made the stand's black darker and rougher with less specular, moved the camera in and brought the posts closer together.\n  2. **test2:** the feet were cut at the bottom and the right foot was still pale. I shrank the feet, re-aimed the camera, lowered the fill and lifted the rim.\n  3. **Final:** both earrings are whole, sharp and warm yellow, not rose. The right foot still carries a grey highlight; I did not do a fourth render to fix it.\n\n## Checks run and not verified\n- **Checked:** output sizes were read back with Pillow and from file sizes, and every render was viewed.\n- **Not verified:** colour on a calibrated display, and how the images look in the site's layout.",
